<section class="content">

<h2>Catechist's Guide</h2>
<p class="big-question">For preparing and leading the class. For catechist use only.</p>

<h3>Class Summary</h3>

<p><strong>Estimated duration:</strong> 70-75 minutes</p>
<p><strong>Central theme:</strong> The sacrament of Reconciliation as a living encounter with God's mercy: the Father's embrace of the son who returns. Not a courtroom, but a homecoming feast.</p>
<p><strong>Objectives:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Understand that Christ himself, on the day of the Resurrection, gave the Church the power to forgive sins (Jn 20:22-23).</li>
<li>Know the various names of the sacrament and what each emphasizes (CIC 1422-1424).</li>
<li>Master the three acts of the penitent (contrition, confession, satisfaction) and absolution.</li>
<li>Overcome the fear, shame, and obstacles that prevent confession.</li>
<li>Learn the practical steps for making a good confession.</li>
<li>Connect the parable of the prodigal son with the sacrament.</li>
<li>Understand the absolute nature of the sacramental seal and its importance.</li>
<li>Value frequent confession as a means of sanctification.</li>
<li>Explain simply what an indulgence is and how it relates to the sacrament.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key verses:</strong> "Receive the Holy Spirit. If you forgive the sins of any, they are forgiven; if you retain the sins of any, they are retained" (Jn 20:22-23); "If we confess our sins, he who is faithful and just will forgive us our sins and cleanse us from all unrighteousness" (1 Jn 1:9).</p>
<p><strong>Catechism references:</strong> CCC 1420-1498.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Suggested Class Structure</h3>

<table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.85rem;margin:16px 0">
<tr style="background:var(--gold-light)">
<th style="padding:8px;text-align:left;border:1px solid var(--border)">Section</th>
<th style="padding:8px;text-align:left;border:1px solid var(--border)">Duration</th>
<th style="padding:8px;text-align:left;border:1px solid var(--border)">What the catechist does</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">1. The Great Question: Rembrandt's embrace</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">10 min</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">Project or show an image of Rembrandt's <em>The Return of the Prodigal Son</em>. Narrate the parable (Lk 15:11-24). Ask: "Is there anything God cannot forgive? Is there something you believe is unforgivable?" Let the silence sit. Then: "This sacrament is the answer to that question."</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">2. The names of the sacrament</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">5 min</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">List the five names. Each illuminates something distinct. Have the group repeat after you: "It's not a courtroom; it's a feast." Emphasize the richness of having multiple names for one reality.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">3. Christ institutes the sacrament</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">10 min</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">Read Jn 20:19-23 aloud, slowly. Emphasize the context: Easter evening, locked doors, terrified disciples, glorified wounds. Explain <em>in persona Christi</em> with a concrete image: the priest is like a window &mdash;you don't look at the glass, you look through it. Mention Mt 16:19, the keys, and Jas 5:16.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">4. The acts of the penitent</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">12 min</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">Contrition (perfect/imperfect; both valid &mdash; this is a relief to many). Confession (integral, in kind and number, why speaking aloud matters). Satisfaction (reparation, medicine, not punishment). Absolution: the moment God erases everything. Use the window analogy: you broke the window (guilt), absolution forgives the guilt, satisfaction fixes the glass.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">5. The seal of confession</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">5 min</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">Absolute, inviolable, without exceptions. Tell the story of St. John Nepomucene. Say directly: "The priest would rather die than reveal what you say. Some have. This is the safest place on earth."</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">6. Practical guide for confession + mortal vs. venial</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">10 min</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">Walk through the ten practical steps. Teach the Act of Contrition. Explain examinations of conscience. Clarify mortal vs. venial sin: three conditions for mortal, what must be confessed vs. what may be confessed. If the group is small, do a mock confession (format only).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">7. Scriptural Connection + Cultural Richness</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">5 min</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">Read Lk 15:20 (the father runs) and 2 Cor 5:18-20 (we are ambassadors of reconciliation). Touch briefly on the saints, Rembrandt, and Nouwen. Give book recommendations.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">8. For Reflection + Closing</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">8 min</td>
<td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--border)">Choose 1-2 questions. Silent reflection, then pair work. Never force sharing. Read the Key Idea. Pray together. Remind them of parish confession times. If possible, arrange confessions at the end of the session or the following week.</td>
</tr>
